Instead of building rotated strings, this checks each possible starting position with indexes. The expression `(start + offset) % n` wraps around when the end of `goal` is reached.

The input strings are not changed. If the strings are empty, they are equal after the length check, so the method returns true before using modulo by zero.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
    bool rotateString(string& s,string& goal){
        if (s.size() != goal.size()) {
            return false;
        }

        int n = static_cast<int>(s.size());
        if (n == 0) {
            return true;
        }

        for (int start = 0; start < n; start++) {
            bool matches = true;

            for (int offset = 0; offset < n; offset++) {
                if (s[(start + offset) % n] != goal[offset]) {
                    matches = false;
                    break;
                }
            }

            if (matches) {
                return true;
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
};
```

A quick dry run with `s = "abcde"` and `goal = "deabc"` uses start index 3. The circular characters are `d, e, a, b, c`, so that starting point succeeds.

Time complexity: O(n^2) in the worst case.
Space complexity: O(1), apart from a few variables.

Every left rotation can be made by cutting `s` at a position and joining the suffix before the prefix. Trying all cut positions gives a direct way to check the possible results.

For `s = "abcd"`, cutting at 2 creates `"cd" + "ab"`, which is `"cdab"`. The original order is included by using cut position 0. The input itself stays unchanged.

```csharp
public class Solution
{
    public bool rotateString(string s, string goal)
    {
        if (s.Length != goal.Length)
        {
            return false;
        }

        for (int cut = 0; cut < s.Length; cut++)
        {
            string rotated = s.Substring(cut) + s.Substring(0, cut);
            if (rotated == goal)
            {
                return true;
            }
        }

        return s.Length == 0;
    }
}
```

Dry run: with `s = "abc"` and `goal = "cab"`, cut 2 produces `"c" + "ab"`, so the method returns true. Different lengths are rejected before any substring is created.

Time complexity: O(n^2), because up to n rebuilt strings of length n are checked.
Space complexity: O(n) extra space at one time for the temporary rotated string.

The doubled-string idea still helps here, but the search is written with KMP instead of a library search. The LPS table tells the pattern where to continue after a mismatch, so already matched characters are not checked again.

The code scans at most twice the length of `s`. It does not modify the input strings. The empty pattern is handled in the helper so two empty strings return true.

```go
func buildLPS(pattern string) []int {
    lps := make([]int, len(pattern))
    length := 0
    index := 1

    for index < len(pattern) {
        if pattern[index] == pattern[length] {
            length++
            lps[index] = length
            index++
        } else if length > 0 {
            length = lps[length-1]
        } else {
            index++
        }
    }

    return lps
}

func containsKMP(text, pattern string) bool {
    if len(pattern) == 0 {
        return true
    }

    lps := buildLPS(pattern)
    textIndex := 0
    patternIndex := 0

    for textIndex < len(text) {
        if text[textIndex] == pattern[patternIndex] {
            textIndex++
            patternIndex++

            if patternIndex == len(pattern) {
                return true
            }
        } else if patternIndex > 0 {
            patternIndex = lps[patternIndex-1]
        } else {
            textIndex++
        }
    }

    return false
}

func rotateString(strS, goal string) bool {
    if len(strS) != len(goal) {
        return false
    }

    return containsKMP(strS+strS, goal)
}
```

Time complexity: O(n), including the LPS construction and the search.
Space complexity: O(n) for the doubled text and the LPS slice.

This version does the rotations directly. Each loop moves the first character to the end, then compares the changed string with `goal`.

The length check avoids doing any work when the strings cannot match. The empty string is also covered explicitly, since the rotation loop would otherwise have no chance to return true.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean rotateString(String s, String goal) {
        if (s.length() != goal.length()) {
            return false;
        }

        if (s.length() == 0) {
            return true;
        }

        String current = s;
        for (int rotation = 0; rotation < s.length(); rotation++) {
            if (current.equals(goal)) {
                return true;
            }

            current = current.substring(1) + current.charAt(0);
        }

        return false;
    }
}
```

For `s = "abc"` and `goal = "bca"`, the checks are `abc`, then `bca`, so the method returns true.

Time complexity: O(n^2), since there can be n rotations and each comparison or new string takes O(n).
Space complexity: O(n) for the current rotated string.

A rotation keeps the same length, so that check comes first. After that, `s + s` contains every possible starting position of `s`. If `goal` appears inside it, it can be made by rotating `s`.

For example, `abcde + abcde` contains `cdeab`. This also handles the empty string because two empty strings have equal length and the containment check is true.

```python
class Solution:
    def rotateString(self, s, goal):
        if len(s) != len(goal):
            return False
        return goal in (s + s)
```

Time complexity: O(n^2) in the worst case for the substring search.
Space complexity: O(n), because `s + s` creates a new string.

