# NOTE : 
> ```string::npos``` is a special constant used by string functions to indicate "not found".

Intuition

The key idea is that if one string is a rotation of another, then it must appear as a substring when the original string is concatenated with itself. By doubling the string, all possible rotations are represented as contiguous substrings of the same length.

Approach

First, concatenate the string with itself. Then, iterate through this combined string and check whether any substring of length equal to the original string matches the target string. If a match is found, the target is a valid rotation.

Solution
class Solution{	
	public:
		bool rotateString(string& s, string& goal){
			string check = s + s;
			int n = s.length();
			for(int i = 0; i < n; i++){
				if(check.substr(i, n) == goal) return true;
			}
			return false;
		}
};

Complexity

Time Complexity:
The algorithm runs in O(n^2) time in the worst case, since for each of the n starting positions, a substring comparison of length n is performed.

Space Complexity:
The space complexity is O(n) , as an additional string of length 2n is created to store the concatenated result.
